1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,592
Reed beds near the road to Multan. A young
prince, shaking with fever, has thrown

2
00:00:05,592 --> 00:00:10,502
his club and missed. His spear is in the
lion's chest, and his arms are too weak to

3
00:00:10,502 --> 00:00:12,040
hold it. The spear breaks.

4
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:27,169
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Six. Today

5
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:31,947
there are no plots in the palace and no
letters in the chancery. Today we go out

6
00:00:31,947 --> 00:00:35,770
into the reed beds, where something large is
moving in the dark.

7
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:41,816
A prince with a fever. A spear in his hand.
And a lion that does not fall. This is a

8
00:00:41,816 --> 00:00:46,330
true scene, written down by a man who served
that prince for many years.

9
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:06,346
First, let me remind you who our prince is.
His name was Masud. He was the son of

10
00:01:06,346 --> 00:01:11,140
Mahmud of Ghazni, the great conqueror, and in
the year ten thirty he became sultan

11
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:15,759
himself, after the struggle with his brother
that we followed in Folios Two and

12
00:01:15,759 --> 00:01:16,110
Three.

13
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:21,382
When Beyhaqi wrote his history, he did not
only list battles and dates. He stopped,

14
00:01:21,382 --> 00:01:25,874
here and there, to paint a portrait. And in
one part of his book, he sets down what

15
00:01:25,874 --> 00:01:30,365
kind of man Masud was in his body and in his
habits. He tells us about the sultan's

16
00:01:30,365 --> 00:01:33,450
strength, his hunting, and his stubborn love
of hardship.

17
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:36,850
Today's story comes from those pages.

18
00:01:37,630 --> 00:01:42,409
Now, a word of warning before we begin.
Beyhaqi served Masud for years. He admired

19
00:01:42,409 --> 00:01:47,187
him, and he wrote while Masud's own family
still ruled. So we will listen to these

20
00:01:47,187 --> 00:01:51,966
stories with open ears, but also with a
careful mind. Keep that in your pocket. We

21
00:01:51,966 --> 00:01:53,190
will come back to it.

22
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:13,251
Beyhaqi begins with the young Masud. Even as a
boy, he says, the prince trained his

23
00:02:13,251 --> 00:02:18,032
body hard. He tested his strength. He lifted
heavy stones. He wrestled. He liked to

24
00:02:18,032 --> 00:02:22,870
set traps for waterbirds and other birds, and
he had places built for catching them.

25
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:28,264
Then Beyhaqi adds something he saw with his
own eyes. On days of bitter cold and

26
00:02:28,264 --> 00:02:32,935
heavy snow, Masud would ride out to hunt. He
would get down from his horse in the

27
00:02:32,935 --> 00:02:37,722
snow and stay out in the freezing air for
hours, from one prayer time almost to the

28
00:02:37,722 --> 00:02:38,010
next.

29
00:02:38,290 --> 00:02:42,950
Sometimes, in that cold, he pulled on his
boots with nothing on his feet.

30
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:48,865
Why would a prince do this? Beyhaqi tells us
Masud's own reason. A man must get used

31
00:02:48,865 --> 00:02:53,450
to hardship, he said, so that when a real
crisis comes, he is not helpless.

32
00:02:54,230 --> 00:02:57,310
In Persian, the line is short and plain.

33
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:01,230
Bar chenin chizha khuy bayad kard.

34
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:04,730
One must get used to such things.

35
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:10,158
I like that sentence. It is the voice of a
soldier, not a poet. Hold on to it,

36
00:03:10,158 --> 00:03:15,104
because the rest of this story is about a man
who trained himself for every kind of

37
00:03:15,104 --> 00:03:17,130
danger. Or at least, for one kind.

38
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:35,190
Then Beyhaqi turns to lions.

39
00:03:35,970 --> 00:03:40,712
In those days there were lions in Iran, and in
the lands that are now Afghanistan

40
00:03:40,712 --> 00:03:45,571
and Pakistan. They lived in the thickets and
reed beds along the rivers. Today they

41
00:03:45,571 --> 00:03:50,489
are gone from all of those places. But in
Masud's time, a lion hunt was the greatest

42
00:03:50,489 --> 00:03:52,070
test a nobleman could face.

43
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:57,384
Most princes hunted lions the safe way. Their
servants and soldiers did the

44
00:03:57,384 --> 00:04:02,401
dangerous work, and the prince took the glory.
Masud, Beyhaqi tells us, did not. He

45
00:04:02,401 --> 00:04:06,210
hunted lions alone, and he would not let his
servants help him.

46
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:11,621
Beyhaqi lists the places where he did it,
across the eastern lands, from the region

47
00:04:11,621 --> 00:04:14,650
of Herat in the west down toward Bust and
Ghazni.

48
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:20,368
He also tells us exactly how Masud did it. The
prince carried two weapons. One was a

49
00:04:20,368 --> 00:04:25,130
short, heavy throwing weapon, something like a
club or a short javelin. The other

50
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:28,070
was a short, thick spear. First he threw the
club.

51
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:32,997
If that did not stop the lion, he planted his
spear and took the lion's charge on

52
00:04:32,997 --> 00:04:37,816
its point, and held it there while the animal
twisted and fought, until it grew weak

53
00:04:37,816 --> 00:04:42,463
and fell. Only with the fiercest lions did he
let his men come in with swords and

54
00:04:42,463 --> 00:04:42,750
axes.

55
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:48,634
It is a terrifying method. Everything depends
on the strength of one man's arms, at

56
00:04:48,634 --> 00:04:52,570
the moment when a lion is throwing its whole
weight against him.

57
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:10,730
Now comes the scene I promised you.

58
00:05:11,510 --> 00:05:16,179
It happened before Masud was sultan. Beyhaqi
tells us that, for a time, his father

59
00:05:16,179 --> 00:05:20,961
Mahmud was displeased with him, and the young
prince was sent away toward Multan, in

60
00:05:20,961 --> 00:05:25,459
what is now Pakistan. Why was Mahmud angry?
Beyhaqi says only that it is a long

61
00:05:25,459 --> 00:05:28,590
story, and he does not tell it here. So
neither will I.

62
00:05:29,370 --> 00:05:34,172
On that journey, near a place called Kikanan,
Masud met a lion. And he was ill. He

63
00:05:34,172 --> 00:05:39,033
had what was called a quartan fever, a fever
that comes back again and again, every

64
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:41,610
few days, and leaves a man weak and shaking.

65
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:46,550
He did not turn back. He went at the lion in
his usual way.

66
00:05:47,330 --> 00:05:51,670
He threw his club. But the lion ducked, and
the club flew over its head.

67
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:57,005
So he took the charge on his spear, as he had
done so many times before. The spear

68
00:05:57,005 --> 00:06:01,671
went into the lion's chest. But this time the
fever had taken his strength. He could

69
00:06:01,671 --> 00:06:05,170
not hold it. The lion broke the spear and came
straight at him.

70
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:11,257
Stop for a moment and picture it. A sick young
man, alone, his club gone, his spear

71
00:06:11,257 --> 00:06:14,070
in pieces, and a wounded lion on top of him.

72
00:06:14,850 --> 00:06:16,090
What did he do?

73
00:06:16,870 --> 00:06:21,799
Beyhaqi tells us that Masud struck the lion
with his bare hands, both hands, on the

74
00:06:21,799 --> 00:06:26,728
head and the face. The lion went down. Masud
held it down, and shouted for his men.

75
00:06:26,728 --> 00:06:30,410
One of his guards ran in and finished the
animal with a sword.

76
00:06:31,190 --> 00:06:36,228
Everyone who saw it was astonished. And
Beyhaqi says they agreed on one thing. The

77
00:06:36,228 --> 00:06:41,204
old story of Bahram Gur, the story they had
all read in books, must be true after

78
00:06:41,204 --> 00:06:41,450
all.

79
00:06:56,730 --> 00:07:01,412
Who was Bahram Gur? He was a king of ancient
Persia, of the Sasanian family, who

80
00:07:01,412 --> 00:07:06,211
lived some six hundred years before Masud. In
Persian poetry and legend, he is the

81
00:07:06,211 --> 00:07:10,952
great hunter king. The most famous tale says
that, to win his throne, he took the

82
00:07:10,952 --> 00:07:13,410
royal crown from between two hungry lions.

83
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:18,984
So the men around Masud were saying something
quite bold. We always thought those

84
00:07:18,984 --> 00:07:23,956
stories were exaggerated. Now we have seen it
with our own eyes. Our prince is a new

85
00:07:23,956 --> 00:07:24,370
Bahram.

86
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:43,030
Beyhaqi has two more lion stories for us.

87
00:07:43,810 --> 00:07:48,686
The second happened later, in India. This time
Masud was hunting from the back of an

88
00:07:48,686 --> 00:07:53,447
elephant. As was the custom, the elephant's
head was covered with iron. Suddenly a

89
00:07:53,447 --> 00:07:56,930
big lion burst out of the thicket and attacked
the elephant.

90
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:02,076
Masud threw his club, and wounded the lion in
the chest. But the lion was not

91
00:08:02,076 --> 00:08:06,840
finished. It leapt onto the elephant from
behind. The great animal was shaking under

92
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:11,490
him. Masud went down on one knee, on the
elephant's back, and struck once with his

93
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:11,830
sword.

94
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:16,989
Beyhaqi says that single blow cut off both of
the lion's front legs. The lion fell

95
00:08:16,989 --> 00:08:21,570
and died. Those who were there said they had
never heard of anything like it.

96
00:08:22,350 --> 00:08:27,103
The third story is about numbers. Before he
took the throne, Masud went out on a

97
00:08:27,103 --> 00:08:31,796
day's hunt on the way toward Herat. That day,
Beyhaqi tells us, he killed eight

98
00:08:31,796 --> 00:08:34,470
lions, and caught a ninth alive with a lasso.

99
00:08:49,750 --> 00:08:53,310
And here, our narrator steps into his own
story.

100
00:08:54,090 --> 00:08:58,808
That evening, back at the tent, there was
wine, and everyone talked about the lions

101
00:08:58,808 --> 00:09:03,583
and praised the prince. A courtier asked for
paper and ink, and on the spot he wrote

102
00:09:03,583 --> 00:09:08,188
a poem in Arabic in Masud's honor. The
courtier was Bu Sahl Zawzani. If you heard

103
00:09:08,188 --> 00:09:09,950
Folio Five, you know that name.

104
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:14,090
He was the clever, bitter rival of Beyhaqi's
teacher.

105
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:19,515
Masud liked the poem, and it was copied out.
Beyhaqi tells us that he was standing

106
00:09:19,515 --> 00:09:24,272
right there, and that he copied it too. But
then, he says, he lost his copy over the

107
00:09:24,272 --> 00:09:29,030
years. So in his book he writes down only the
lines he still remembers, and he tells

108
00:09:29,030 --> 00:09:29,370
us so.

109
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:34,695
That small confession is pure Beyhaqi. He
could have filled the page and nobody

110
00:09:34,695 --> 00:09:39,470
would have known. Instead he tells us,
plainly, this is from memory, and some of it

111
00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:39,930
is gone.

112
00:09:40,710 --> 00:09:45,319
Then he adds a comment. There was an old
Arabic saying about poets. The best poetry

113
00:09:45,319 --> 00:09:49,873
is the most false. In other words, the most
beautiful praise is usually a lie. But

114
00:09:49,873 --> 00:09:54,371
not here, says Beyhaqi. The prince really did
have all these qualities, and more.

115
00:09:54,371 --> 00:09:56,370
The poet did not need to exaggerate.

116
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:15,510
So now, let us take that careful mind out of
our pocket.

117
00:10:16,290 --> 00:10:20,721
Is Beyhaqi flattering his old master here?
Perhaps a little. He loved Masud, he

118
00:10:20,721 --> 00:10:25,377
served him, and he wrote under Masud's family.
But notice what he does. He tells us

119
00:10:25,377 --> 00:10:30,089
where these things happened. He tells us which
things he saw himself. He admits what

120
00:10:30,089 --> 00:10:30,650
he forgot.

121
00:10:30,930 --> 00:10:35,839
And he leaves out the story of Mahmud's anger
rather than tell it badly. That is not

122
00:10:35,839 --> 00:10:39,930
how a flatterer writes. That is how a witness
writes, even a fond one.

123
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:45,511
And here is the deeper point. Beyhaqi does not
only show us Masud the lion killer.

124
00:10:45,511 --> 00:10:50,428
In other parts of the same book, as we heard
in Folio Five, he shows us a sultan who

125
00:10:50,428 --> 00:10:55,346
did not always listen to good advice. A sultan
who could be stubborn, proud and sure

126
00:10:55,346 --> 00:10:55,990
of himself.

127
00:10:56,770 --> 00:11:01,421
Put those two pictures side by side, and you
see the whole man. Masud trained his

128
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:06,186
body for every hardship. He would face a lion
alone, sick, with his bare hands. But

129
00:11:06,186 --> 00:11:10,779
a lion is a single enemy, in a single moment,
and courage alone can beat it. The

130
00:11:10,779 --> 00:11:13,650
dangers that were coming for Masud were
different.

131
00:11:13,930 --> 00:11:18,945
Slow, patient horsemen from the steppe.
Quarrels among his own generals. Hard

132
00:11:18,945 --> 00:11:21,810
choices that needed listening, not strength.

133
00:11:22,590 --> 00:11:27,806
Bar chenin chizha khuy bayad kard. One must
get used to such things. Masud got used

134
00:11:27,806 --> 00:11:32,959
to snow and fever and lions. Whether he ever
got used to hearing a hard truth is a

135
00:11:32,959 --> 00:11:35,850
question Beyhaqi lets us answer for ourselves.

136
00:11:51,130 --> 00:11:56,263
If there is one lesson here, it is this:
courage wins the fight in front of you, but

137
00:11:56,263 --> 00:11:58,830
only wisdom sees the fight that is coming.

138
00:11:59,610 --> 00:12:04,991
Next week, we meet one of the old guard of the
empire. Altuntash, the governor of

139
00:12:04,991 --> 00:12:10,306
Khwarazm, a general loyal to Mahmud, suspected
by Masud, and the hero of a story

140
00:12:10,306 --> 00:12:12,830
about loyalty that ends at a deathbed.

141
00:12:13,610 --> 00:12:18,358
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

142
00:12:18,358 --> 00:12:22,994
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

143
00:12:22,994 --> 00:12:27,742
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

144
00:12:27,742 --> 00:12:29,890
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

